    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe check works but it is in the wrong unit, and the codebase already establishes the idiomatic home for exactly this concern.\n\n**1. This belongs as a Bean Validation constraint, not an inline controller guard.**\nThe project has a dedicated `rest/validation` package for field-level input validation, and `PetAgeValidator` already implements the *same* temporal rule this spec asks for:\n\n```java\n// PetAgeValidator.isValid(...)\nif (birthDate.isAfter(today)) { ... \"Birth date cannot be in the future\" ... return false; }\n```\n\n\"Reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date\" is the identical pattern (`isAfter(LocalDate.now())`, null tolerated) applied to a different field. A maintainer would expect a parallel constraint \u2014 e.g. a `@RegistrationDateValidation` annotation backed by a validator in `rest/validation`, applied to the `registrationDate` field via the OpenAPI schema (the same way `TelephoneValidation` is wired) \u2014 not a hand-rolled `if` in `addOwner`. The current diff silently diverges from the established mechanism for the same category of logic.\n\n**2. Inconsistent error surface.**\nEvery other input-validation failure in this app flows through `ExceptionControllerAdvice.handleMethodArgumentNotValidException`, producing a structured 400 `ProblemDetail` with `errors` / `schemaValidationErrors` field detail. This change returns a bare `new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)` with no body, so the same client gets two different shapes for the same class of problem. Routing it through Bean Validation would make the 400 response consistent for free.\n\n**3. It further overloads an already catch-all method.**\n`addOwner` has accumulated business-day adjustment, per-day limit, bulk-signup flagging, city capacity, household assignment, identity-key dedup, namesake counting, and auditing. Field-level \"is this date sane\" validation is precisely the concern the validation layer exists to pull *out* of the controller; adding it inline pushes the method further toward being a dumping ground rather than relieving it.\n\nRecommendation: implement the rule as a validation constraint on `registrationDate` mirroring `PetAgeValidator`/`TelephoneValidator`, and drop the inline guard from `addOwner`.",
